FEXCore: 32-bit programs on iOS see "Apple Silicon" and no hypervisor bit

Test for Steam's CEG step (Black Ops 1). On the iOS host FEX reports a
GenuineIntel CPU whose brand string is "Unknown ARM CPU", with the
hypervisor bit set. For the WOW64 module only (HostType Wow64), the brand
is "Apple Silicon" and the hypervisor bit is clear.

Applies on top of 0001. The rebuilt WOW64 module is
app/Madeira/aarch64-windows/xtajit.dll. Not for FEX-Emu.

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CPUID.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CPUID.cpp
index 5b0ea4a..4e964d7 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CPUID.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/CPUID.cpp
@@ -171,6 +171,13 @@ void CPUIDEmu::SetupHostHybridFlag() {
 
     // Truncate to 32-bits, top 32-bits are all reserved in MIDR
     PerCPUData[i].ProductName = ProductNames::ARM_UNKNOWN;
+#ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
+    /* MADEIRA cpu-name test: 32-bit programs on iOS see "Apple Silicon" instead of "Unknown ARM CPU"
+     * (the iOS host publishes one synthetic MIDR that matches no table entry). Black Ops 1's CEG step. */
+    if (CTX->HostFeatures.HostType == FEXCore::HostFeatures::HostTypeEnum::Wow64) {
+      PerCPUData[i].ProductName = ProductNames::ARM_AppleSilicon;
+    }
+#endif
     PerCPUData[i].MIDR = NewMIDR;
     MIDR = NewMIDR;
   }
@@ -454,6 +461,12 @@ FEXCore::CPUID::FunctionResults CPUIDEmu::Function_01h(uint32_t Leaf) const {
 
   // Hypervisor bit is normally set but some applications have issues with it.
   uint32_t Hypervisor = HideHypervisorBit() ? 0 : 1;
+#ifdef FEX_IOS_HOST
+  // MADEIRA cpu-name test: 32-bit programs on iOS see no hypervisor, like most Windows PCs.
+  if (CTX->HostFeatures.HostType == FEXCore::HostFeatures::HostTypeEnum::Wow64) {
+    Hypervisor = 0;
+  }
+#endif
 
   Res.eax = FAMILY_IDENTIFIER;
 
